// woodcore:object heading title
== Types of cracks and causes of cracking
// woodcore:/object title

// woodcore:object text p1
*Surface cracks* (map cracking): with very small widths (< 0.1 mm) they are only an aesthetic limitation and have no influence on watertightness; they can be favourably influenced by curing.
// woodcore:/object p1

// woodcore:object text p2
*Early shrinkage cracks*: concrete heats up as it hardens and contracts as it cools; if this contraction is restrained, through-cracks can appear after only 1 to 3 days. They are influenced by the concrete mix, the fresh-concrete temperature and curing, but hardly at all by reinforcement.
// woodcore:/object p2

// woodcore:object text p3
*Shrinkage cracks*: where shrinkage is restrained (base slabs by friction on the ground, long walls on foundations, floors tied to stiff walls), through-cracks occur; they carry water from about 0.1 mm and may still appear after completion. The reinforcement spreads the shortening over several fine cracks rather than a few wide ones.
// woodcore:/object p3

// woodcore:object text p4
*Temperature cracks*: where the length changes of members subject to temperature are restrained, through-cracks can occur; they cannot be prevented, but their distribution can be improved by the choice of reinforcement.
// woodcore:/object p4

// woodcore:object text p5
*Static cracks* (bending, shear and tension cracks): they occur when the tensile strength of the concrete is exceeded; they are not damage as long as structural safety is met and the crack width does not exceed the agreed requirements.
// woodcore:/object p5

// woodcore:object text p6
*Conclusion*: cracks are a typical feature of concrete construction; they can generally not be prevented, but their consequences can be reduced. The service criteria agreement serves, among other things, to agree the measures with the owner.
// woodcore:/object p6
